          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The normal distribution values seem reasonable. The issue is that when we clip to the maximum bfloat16 value, we're seeing the clip value itself when there are extreme values. Let me check if the issue is actually with negative values:\n\n<tool_call>\n<function=bash>\n<parameter=command>\nexport LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/workspace/python/mlx/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH && python3 -c \"\nimport sys\nsys.path.insert(0, '/workspace/python')\nimport mlx.core as mx\n\n# Let's see what happens with negative uniform values\nprint('Testing negative uniform for bfloat16...')\n\n# Generate uniform samples in (-1, 1)\nlow = mx.array(-0.9999, dtype=mx.bfloat16)\nhigh = mx.array(1.0, dtype=mx.bfloat16)\nuniform_samples = mx.random.uniform(low, high, shape=(100000,))\nprint('uniform max:', mx.max(uniform_samples).item())\nprint('uniform min:', mx.min(uniform_samples).item())\n\n# Test erfinv\nerfinv_samples = mx.erfinv(uniform_samples)\nprint('erfinv max:', mx.max(erfinv_samples).item())\nprint('erfinv min:', mx.min(erfinv_samples).item())\nprint('erfinv has inf:', mx.any(mx.abs(erfinv_samples) == mx.inf).item())\n\n# Check if any erfinv values would overflow bfloat16\nsqrt2 = 1.4142135623730951\nnormal_samples = sqrt2 * erfinv_samples\nprint('sqrt2 * erfinv max:', mx.max(normal_samples).item())\nprint('sqrt2 * erfinv min:', mx.min(normal_samples).item())\nprint('sqrt2 * erfinv has inf:', mx.any(mx.abs(normal_samples) == mx.inf).item())\n\"\n\n</parameter>\n<parameter=description>\nDebug negative erfinv values for bfloat16\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>",
